      "question": {
        ":question/id": "05d80eaf-9239-5ba3-9861-ef51c6826e57",
        ":question/problem": "Calculate the instantaneous rate of change of the function $f(x)=-5tan⁡x$ when $x=\\pi .$",
        ":question/worked-solution": "The instantaneous rate of change  when $x=\\pi$ is given by the value of the derivative ${f}^{′}(x)$ at $x=\\pi .$ \n\nFirst, we compute the derivative, using the fact that $\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(tan⁡x)={sec}^{2}⁡x.$ We get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}^{′}(x) & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(-5tan⁡x) \\\\  & =-5{sec}^{2}⁡x.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nEvaluating at $x=\\pi ,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}^{′}(\\pi ) & =-5{sec}^{2}⁡\\pi  \\\\  & =-5\\cdot (-1)^{2} \\\\  & =-5.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore,  the instantaneous rate of change is $-5.$",
        ":question/difficulty": ":question.difficulty/moderate",
