        ":question/problem": "The derivative of $y=5x-6\\tan x$ when $x=- \\frac{\\pi}{4}$ is",
        ":question/worked-solution": "We know that $\\frac{\\mathrm{d}}{\\mathrm{d}x}(\\tan x)=\\sec^2 x.$ Applying the sum and constant multiple rules, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y'(x)&=\\frac{\\mathrm{d}}{\\mathrm{d}x}\\left(5 x - 6 \\tan x\\right) \\\\ &= -6\\frac{\\mathrm{d}}{\\mathrm{d}x}\\left(\\tan x\\right) + 5\\frac{\\mathrm{d}}{\\mathrm{d}x}\\left(x\\right) \\\\ &= 5 - 6 \\sec^{2}x.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nEvaluating at $x=- \\frac{\\pi}{4},$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y'(- \\frac{\\pi}{4})&=5 - 6 \\sec^{2}\\left(- \\frac{\\pi}{4}\\right) \\\\ &= -7 \\\\ &= -7.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the slope is $-7.$",
